WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:09.540
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt;
Good morning, ladies and gentlemen, and welcome

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.440
to this meeting of the Board of NHS England.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:19.210
In accordance with our time-honoured custom,
we meet in public, and this is a meeting at

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.230
which serious decisions are made.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:31.210
It is not a public meeting, in other words;
we believe that it is important, for the number

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:36.490
of people who have joined us in London this
morning, but also on our live stream, to see

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:42.399
how the Board goes about taking decisions
that affect the investments that we make on

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:46.620
NHS England and on behalf of the public of
England.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:51.530
Everybody is very welcome, and we will turn
now to the business of the meeting.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:56.320
I should explain that the Board work through
a number of different methods.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:02.470
This is the meeting in public of the Board,
but we also have business which is undertaken

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.210
in committees.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:08.010
Those committees then report to the Board;
you will see some of those on the agenda this

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.010
morning.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:14.810
We also have development sessions, in which
the entire Board sits around and works to

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:20.220
develop policy that is from its earliest stages
being worked up into a document that will

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.060
eventually come to a meeting in public of
the Board.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:30.020
Just to give some context to that, yesterday
we spent some considerable time thinking through

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:37.350
our future package for the support of general
practice, to develop a model which would accord

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.340
with the Five Year Forward View as we take
that through.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:47.640
Also, following this meeting, we will go into
private session to deal with issues that are

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.659
strictly confidential and cannot be aired
in public.

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:54.760
Let me first of all take apologies for absence.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:01.229
I only have one, which is Richard Barker,
who is our acting Director for Commissioning

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:02.229
Operations.

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:10.509
To invite the Board to give us indication
of any declarations of interest of matters

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:11.509
on the agenda.

00:02:11.509 --> 00:02:16.060
Victor Adebowale&gt;&gt;Turning Point provide services
in Manchester.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:20.910
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Thank you, Victor.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:29.170
We will take a note of that.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:34.050
I turn now to Chairman’s preliminary remarks,
and my first very pleasant duty is to welcome

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:37.959
two new non-executive directors to the Board
of NHS England.

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:42.010
They are respectively Michelle Mitchell and
Wendy Becker.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:48.670
Michelle has been working for some time in
the voluntary sector, at an earlier career

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:54.570
with Age UK and now as the Chief Executive
Officer of Multiple Sclerosis.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:03:00.220
Wendy Becker joins us as somebody with a very
extensive commercial career, but also currently

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.440
the Deputy Chair of Cancer Research UK.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.870
Can I just say, it is a great pleasure to
welcome you to this first formal meeting of

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:11.100
the Board of NHS England.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:16.229
I have to also mention that this is the last
meeting of Margaret Casely-Hayford, who has

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.160
been on the Board for four years.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.960
I will say a few more words about that at
the end of the meeting under Any Other Business.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.620
Care for Manchester
Next, we are being joined this morning for

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:34.590
item 8, which is the business referring to
the new delegation arrangements and the new

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:39.860
models for Care for Manchester, by the Chief
Executive of Manchester City Council, Sir

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:44.070
Howard Bernstein, who joins us this morning,
and who will join us for our discussion of

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:45.070
that matter.

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:49.930
He is accompanied by Carol Culley, who is
the Deputy City Treasurer.

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:55.850
He will speak to the papers on the new Manchester
arrangements; Paul Baumann will then talk

00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:00.010
to the specific paper that we have on the
delegation arrangements.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:04.590
That is all by way of preliminary business.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:09.070
I turn now to the minutes of the previous
meeting, which I have to thank David Roberts

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:14.070
for chairing that meeting when I was unwell,
and to invite the Board to let me have any

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:17.400
comments they may have on the minutes.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.940
If you are happy then to approve them as a
fair and accurate report of the meeting?

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.200
Okay, thank you very much.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.480
I turn now then to the report of the Chief
Executive.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.220
Simon Stevens&gt;&gt;Thank you, Malcolm.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:36.120
I have, with colleagues, had an opportunity
over the course of the last month to be out

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.120
and about around the country.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:45.960
As usual, I want to thank staff and patients
in Wigan, Grantham, Lincoln, Lancashire, Barking

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:52.639
Havering and at Preston Prison, for their
hospitality.

00:04:52.639 --> 00:04:57.460
In addition to that, obviously an enormous
amount of work has been going on behind the

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:04.900
scenes, both to close out the 2015/2016 year
and to prepare for 2016/2017.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:09.250
Given that we are always onto the next thing,
I do think it is worth the Board just for

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:14.740
a moment taking stock of the pressures but
also the progress that has been made across

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:21.620
the National Health Service in 2015/2016,
today being the last day of the financial

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.270
year.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:28.060
Quite rightly, a lot of attention has been
publicly paid to the pressures in hospitals,

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:32.810
but I think we also have a responsibility
to draw attention to the equally important

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:39.090
but sometimes less well understood work of
GPs, community health teams, mental health

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:45.750
services, social care, voluntary organisations
and, of course, patients’ families and carers

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:47.760
themselves.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.180
That is loosely described sometimes as the
‘out-of-hospital’ care system, a phrase

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:57.740
that I am not sure I particularly like because
it defines the world in rather a stylised

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:04.710
and binary way, but the fact is that the impact
of all of those parts of the health and care

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:12.140
system have has a huge impact on the demand
that is then felt inside the hospital system.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:21.490
Actually, I think contrary to some understanding,
the course of the last year has had some quite

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:27.180
interesting features: emergency admissions
were up by about 1.5% over the course of the

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:36.900
last year, relatively constrained by historical
standards; progress on elective day cases;

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:42.100
and hospital inpatient bed days, actually
about flat to negative so far this year, despite

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:45.690
all of the pressures that people are dealing
with on delayed transfers of care.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:51.900
I think this is a testament to the successful
joint work – again, often unremarked – going

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:58.110
on between hospitals, GP services, social
care and community health services.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:02.801
All of this has obviously been playing out
against a backdrop of a difficult 2015 financial

00:07:02.801 --> 00:07:03.919
year.

00:07:03.919 --> 00:07:10.770
From the point of view of CCGs, and indeed
NHS England, we began the year with a £2

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:18.560
billion real funding increase, of which we
immediately switched £1 billion to local

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.430
authorities to help them deal with their social
care funding pressures through the Better

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:23.430
Care Fund.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:33.590
As Paul will report, we managed a non-recurring
underspend this year of £500 million in order

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:39.570
to contribute to the broader financial pressures
that the Department of Health is on the hook

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:42.860
for, given what is happening in hospital providers.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:50.080
In effect, of that £2 billion, £1.5 billion
is accounted for by the BCF and the managed

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:56.210
underspend, meaning that the entirety of all
the other pressures that CCGs and indeed our

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:59.810
NHS England teams have had to cope with during
the course of this year has been dealt with

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:05.870
with £500 million of extra real-terms purchasing
power, and from that we have obviously funded

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:11.490
routine demand growth, targeted investments
in primary care, local service transformation

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:16.930
programmes like the vanguards and sharp increases
in specialised treatments, including cancer

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:20.650
drugs and NICE-mandated hepatitis C treatments.

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:26.710
Therefore, I do think it is worth the Board
just taking a moment to thank all of our staff

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:31.449
and colleagues who have been involved in the
stewardship of what has been quite a financially

00:08:31.449 --> 00:08:35.800
challenging and complex year in which, nevertheless,
I do think demonstrable progress has been

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:41.750
made on some of our key priorities such as
on the co-produced taskforces on cancer and

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:47.110
mental health, and learning disabilities,
and maternity services, and obesity, and type-B

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:48.110
diabetes.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:53.990
As I say in my report, all of this was overlaid
with the additional stimulation of the general

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:57.010
election and the Comprehensive Spending Review.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:01.870
Thank you to everybody for 2015/2016.

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:10.490
Turning on to 2016/2017 then, the year ahead:
we are today, as we are required to by law,

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:12.470
publishing our business plan for the year.

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:15.400
We have a separate item on the agenda that
covers that.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:21.680
This is both the operational expression of
the requirements that are set for the NHS

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:26.690
by the elected government of the day, but
also, frankly, it summarises the contribution

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:31.910
that we as NHS England will make for the year
ahead.

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:39.600
In my foreword to it, I set out that clearly
there is rightly a lot of focus in the NHS

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:45.490
over the coming year on raising performance
in the areas most visibly under pressure:

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:50.200
A&amp;E, waits for operations and hospital finances.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:55.480
However, I do think it will also be critically
important that, at the same time, we make

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:02.110
progress on some other areas, including strengthening
GP care, making progress on mental health

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:03.110
and prevention.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:07.839
In order to square that circle, it seems to
me that three of the guiding principles that

00:10:07.839 --> 00:10:11.790
should shape our work are that we need to
stick with the shared plan that we have, constancy

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:15.100
of purpose and priorities rather than chopping
and changing.

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:21.830
That is why sharp-eyed readers will notice
strong continuity in the priorities in our

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:26.060
2016/2017 business plan with those from the
past year, and indeed the year before.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:30.710
That is because these are big, fundamental
challenges that require action over years,

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:33.020
not months.

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:36.490
Stick with the plan, constancy of purpose
and priorities.

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:40.950
Coherent national support between all of the
various national bodies, including ourselves,

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:46.690
NHS Improvement, other national bodies such
as HEE and Public Health England, to work

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:54.040
locally to support improvement, and the 44
sustainability and transformation planning

00:10:54.040 --> 00:11:00.740
processes which are now being kicked off are
part of how we intend to give life to the

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:04.370
local versions of the Five Year Forward View
now through 2020.

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:06.870
Solving today’s problems with tomorrow’s
solutions

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:11.750
That relates to my third point, which is that
I think we have to partly solve today’s

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:14.990
issues by getting on with tomorrow’s solutions.

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:19.320
It is not the case that in the name of stability,
tempting as it might be, we can just think

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:22.220
that this year ahead can be a year of better
firefighting.

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:26.279
Actually, a lot of the solutions to the real
pressures that people are experiencing right

00:11:26.279 --> 00:11:32.140
now lie in the change programme that we have
collectively set out for the years ahead.

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:37.260
So if you think about the pressure on A&amp;Es,
then part of the answer there, of course,

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:42.560
is a redesigned urgent and emergency care
system spanning the out-of-hospital services

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:48.970
as well as the in-hospital services, including
111, GP out-of-hours procurement, community

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:50.440
and social care.

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:56.860
Part of the answer in primary care, of course,
lies in boundary-spanning work, bigger, multi-disciplinary

00:11:56.860 --> 00:12:01.149
primary care teams, technology-enabled care,
the sorts of things that we are going to be

00:12:01.149 --> 00:12:06.970
talking about in the new GP road map that
we intend to publish very shortly.

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:13.340
An enormous programme of work in 2016/2017,
but a sense that the direction is clear.

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:17.600
A number of things to land in the next two
or three weeks that Paul will describe in

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:26.950
his report in terms of the reconciliation
of the activity levels, the funding flows,

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:32.690
the link to the £1.8 billion sustainability
fund, so a lot of work to be done in the next

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:38.010
few weeks, but nevertheless a sense that the
direction is now clear.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:46.830
Finally, I would just mention, Malcolm, the
fact that we warmly supported the Budget announcement

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:53.390
around the so-called ‘sugar tax’, and
the I think announcement yesterday by Irn

00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:57.810
Bru that it is already setting in place the
process of reformulation.

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:05.170
It is exactly the kind of action that we want
to see more broadly from the sugary fizzy

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:07.970
drinks suppliers.

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:13.459
If we get this right, together with a much
wider package of action on reformulation,

00:13:13.459 --> 00:13:19.520
on promotions, on advertising and marketing,
combined action by schools, the food industry,

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:25.130
local authorities, the NHS and government,
then this augurs well for progress on childhood

00:13:25.130 --> 00:13:27.040
obesity in this country.

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:30.330
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt;Thank you very much, Simon.

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:37.240
Let me invite any comments or questions from
the Board to the Chief Executive.

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:39.190
No?

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:41.140
Okay.

00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:42.180
Just two things, I think.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:47.990
First of all, to endorse what Simon said about
our thanks to all of those who have helped

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:50.090
us come through an immensely difficult year.

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:53.649
I always find at this time of the year we
are always reflecting on how really difficult

00:13:53.649 --> 00:13:58.790
last year was, knowing that next year is going
to be much more challenging, and we will be

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:02.830
dealing with that in a lot of the other business
that is coming to the Board today, but we

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:05.830
would include obviously you and your team
in our thanks, Simon.

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:07.790
Thank you for all of that.

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:12.360
Secondly, just please to ask the Board to
note formally the content of the annex to

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:17.300
Simon’s report, which is a report of the
use of the corporate seal by NHS England over

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:18.560
the past year.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:19.170
Okay?

